content.text:
I fixed it: the `background-color` inside the nested `@media` now gets wrapped in `&[data-v-…]` like the outer rule, so the media query applies again.

**Cause:** when a scoped rule contains nested rules, its own declarations get moved into a `&` rule so they can be scoped. That only happened at the top level of the rule. Declarations directly inside a nested at-rule (`@media`, `@container`, etc.) were left bare, so they never got the scope attribute and lost to the scoped outer rule.

**Fix**, in `packages/compiler-sfc/src/style/pluginScoped.ts`: I moved the wrapping into a small helper, `extractAndWrapNodes`. It now runs on the rule itself and on each at-rule directly inside it. It also moves comments along with the declarations so they stay next to them.

The issue's example now compiles to:
```css
.foo {
&[data-v-test] { background-color: red }
.bar[data-v-test] {}
@media only screen and (max-width: 800px) {
&[data-v-test] { background-color: green }
.x[data-v-test]{color:red}
}
}
```

The existing `compileStyle` tests all pass (25). I checked the output above with a throwaway test that I then deleted, so the change adds no test of its own. At-rules nested more than one level inside the rule (an `@media` inside an `@media`) are not wrapped.

